    <!-- ===== Interactive demos: type inference ===== -->
    <string name="demo_tip_basliq">Type inference lab</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_tesvir">When you write val x = … Kotlin looks at the right-hand side and works out the type itself.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_kod_serhi">what the compiler sees:</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_cixarilan">Inferred type: </string>
    <string name="demo_tip_bos">Type a value above or pick one of the examples.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_long_l">The L suffix means Long (64 bits).</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_long_boyuk">The value is bigger than the Int range, so Long was chosen automatically.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_int">Whole numbers are Int by default (32 bits).</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_float">The f suffix means Float (32-bit decimal).</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_double">Decimal numbers are Double by default (64 bits).</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_boolean">A logical value: it can only be true or false.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_char">Single quotes mean Char, double quotes mean String.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_string">Text between double quotes is a String.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_null">On its own, null has the type Nothing?. Usually you write the type explicitly: val x: String? = null</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_bos_siyahi">An empty list\'s element type is unknown — write it explicitly: listOf&lt;Int&gt;()</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_list">listOf() returns a read-only list.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_mutable_list">mutableListOf() allows adding and removing elements.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_map">The form mapOf(\"a\" to 1) gives Map&lt;String, Int&gt;.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_set">A Set doesn\'t keep duplicate elements.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_array">An Array has a fixed size; a List is usually more convenient.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_lambda">Curly braces are a lambda; with no parameters its type is () -&gt; Unit.</string>
    <string name="demo_tip_taninmayan">Unrecognized expression — pick one of the examples above.</string>

    <!-- ===== Interactive demos: null safety ===== -->
    <string name="demo_ns_basliq">Null safety lab</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_tesvir">Make the value null or non-null, then pick an operator — see the result and the explanation right away.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_deyer_var">Has a value (null when off)</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_kompilyasiya">COMPILATION ERROR</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_cokme">RUNTIME CRASH</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_netice">OUTPUT</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_ne_bas_verdi">What happened?</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_hec_ne">(nothing was printed)</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_direct">Kotlin won\'t even let this code run. The error is caught at compile time, not at runtime — so the user will never see it.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_safe_null">The safe call operator (?.): if the receiver is null, the call is skipped entirely and the result is null. No crash.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_safe_dolu">The receiver isn\'t null, so length is computed as usual.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_bang_null">The !! operator means “I guarantee this isn\'t null”. If you\'re wrong — NPE. Avoid it whenever you can.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_bang_dolu">This time the value wasn\'t null, so it worked. But !! is still risky — next time it might be null.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_elvis_null">The Elvis operator (?:) returns the fallback value on the right when the left side is null.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_elvis_dolu">The left side isn\'t null, so the right side of the Elvis operator never runs.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_let_null">The let block runs only when the receiver isn\'t null. When it is null, the whole block is skipped.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_let_dolu">Inside the block, it is already smart-cast to String — no extra ? needed.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_if_null">The condition was false, so the block didn\'t run.</string>
    <string name="demo_ns_if_dolu">SMART CAST: after the null check the compiler automatically treats ad as String — no need to write ?.</string>

